"""Focused walkthroughs for hardware concepts needing their own worked example."""
from . import T

T('1-boolean','sop-rows','SOP','Build a canonical SOP from true input rows',r'''
A sum of products represents a Boolean function by ORing several AND terms. In a canonical SOP, every product contains every input variable exactly once. Such a product is a minterm: it is true for precisely one complete input assignment. With A as the most significant bit, minterm index five means input 101 for variables A, B and C. A zero in that row produces a complemented literal; a one produces an ordinary literal.
Construct a canonical SOP by listing the rows where the output must be one, translating each row into its minterm and ORing the results. Required-zero rows contribute no terms. A simplified SOP may omit variables, but that simplification is a separate step; the canonical form records all specified true rows explicitly.
Check each proposed product against its source row before combining them. Swapping the variable order changes the numeric indices. Also distinguish canonical SOP from canonical POS: POS is constructed from false rows using maxterms and reverses the literal convention. The safest verification is to determine exactly which rows make each product one and compare their union with the required true-row set.
''','For variable order A,B,C, construct the canonical SOP for F=Σm(1,2,7).',r'Expand indices::1=001,\quad2=010,\quad7=111::A is the high-order bit, so these are the required true assignments.~Translate rows::m_1=\bar A\bar BC,\quad m_2=\bar AB\bar C,\quad m_7=ABC::A product uses a complemented literal exactly where its row has a zero.~Combine products::F=\bar A\bar BC+\bar AB\bar C+ABC::OR includes every required true row and no other row.', 'F=¬A¬BC+¬AB¬C+ABC.','The three products are true only at 001, 010 and 111 respectively; their union is exactly {1,2,7}.')

T('1-kmap-tabular','prime-chart','Prime implicants','Use a prime-implicant chart to identify essential terms',r'''
An implicant is a product term whose covered input rows all belong to the function’s true set, or to allowed don’t-care rows. A prime implicant is an implicant that cannot be enlarged by removing another literal without covering a required zero. Being prime does not mean a term must appear in every minimum expression. Essential prime implicants have the stronger property that they alone cover at least one required minterm.
After generating all prime implicants, construct a chart with one column for every required true minterm and one row for each prime. Mark the minterms each prime covers. Any column having only one mark forces that row into the expression. Remove all columns covered by selected essentials and solve any remaining cover according to the stated cost, such as number of terms followed by number of literals.
Don’t-care columns are not required in the final chart even though they can help form larger primes. A chosen prime may overlap another chosen prime; overlapping coverage is legal. Verify the cover both for completeness and for exclusion of required zeros, because merely covering all listed ones does not justify a term that also turns a specified zero into one.
''','For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,5), the prime implicants are ¬A¬B, ¬A¬C and ¬BC. Find the essential primes and a minimum SOP.',r'Build coverage::\bar A\bar B\to\{0,1\},\quad\bar A\bar C\to\{0,2\},\quad\bar BC\to\{1,5\}::Translate each product into the exact rows it covers.~Find unique columns::::Minterm 2 is covered only by ¬A¬C; minterm 5 is covered only by ¬BC. Both terms are essential.~Finish cover::F=\bar A\bar C+\bar BC::Those two essentials also cover 0 and 1, so no remaining column needs ¬A¬B.', 'The essential primes are ¬A¬C and ¬BC; their sum is a minimum two-term SOP.','Their covered sets unite to {0,1,2,5}. Both are forced by unique columns, so a one-term cover is impossible and the third prime is redundant.')

T('1-combinational','decoder-minterms','Decoders','Realize minterms with an enabled decoder',r'''
A decoder converts an n-bit input code into one selected output among 2ⁿ possibilities. For an enabled active-high decoder, output Yᵢ is one exactly when the binary input value is i; every other output is zero. Thus each output already implements a canonical minterm. A decoder is combinational: changing its inputs changes the selected output after propagation delay, with no stored previous input.
To implement a Boolean function, assign its variables to the decoder inputs in the specified bit order and combine the outputs corresponding to the function’s true minterms. Active-high outputs normally feed an OR gate. With active-low decoder outputs, the appropriate combining logic is often a NAND because the selected line goes low; the active levels must be stated rather than guessed. An enable pin can disable every output, so it must also be driven to its asserted level.
Trace at least one selected input and one unselected input to verify the wiring. Using the correct output numbers with reversed input order realizes a different function. A decoder does not choose a data input as a multiplexer does: its output identity is determined by the input code itself, which makes minterm realization a direct connection between a truth table and hardware.
''','Implement F(A,B,C)=Σm(1,2,7) using an active-high 3-to-8 decoder with an active-high enable. A is the most significant input.',r'Assign input order::i=4A+2B+C::Wire A, B and C to the high, middle and low decoder inputs respectively.~Select minterms::F=Y_1+Y_2+Y_7::Connect outputs 1, 2 and 7 to a three-input OR gate and tie enable to one.~Trace enabled inputs::::For 010 only Y₂ is one, so F=1. For 011 only Y₃ is one, so all three OR inputs are zero and F=0.', 'Assert enable and OR decoder outputs Y₁, Y₂ and Y₇.','For every enabled input exactly one decoder output is high, so the OR is one precisely at indices 1, 2 and 7.')

T('1-sequential','moore-detector','Moore','Trace a Moore machine’s registered output state',r'''
A Moore machine associates its output with its current state. The next state still depends on the current state and current input, but an input alone does not directly determine the output. In a synchronous implementation, input sampling changes the state at a clock edge and the output then follows the newly registered state. It is therefore important to state whether a trace reports values before or after each sampling edge.
For a sequence detector, define each state by the relevant suffix of the input history. A state can remember no useful prefix, a partial match, or a completed match. A separate completed-match state produces the Moore output. When overlapping matches are allowed, the next transition must preserve any suffix that is also a prefix of the target pattern; returning blindly to the start can miss a later match.
Compared with a Mealy detector, a Moore design often uses an extra output state, but latency comparisons depend on the observation convention. Do not assert an extra cycle without specifying when each output is observed. Verify a state table by tracing both a successful pattern and a near miss, computing every next state from the state that existed before the edge.
''','A Moore detector for 01 starts in N (output 0). Z means the last bit was 0 (output 0); M means 01 has just completed (output 1). On 0, every state goes to Z; on 1, Z goes to M while N and M go to N. Trace inputs 00101, reporting output after each edge.',r'Trace first two zeros::::N→Z→Z. Each zero preserves the useful suffix 0, and Z has output zero.~Complete first match::::The third input is 1, so Z→M and the registered-state output becomes one.~Continue overlapping search::::The fourth input 0 sends M→Z; the fifth input 1 sends Z→M. The final two outputs are zero then one.', 'After-edge states are Z,Z,M,Z,M; outputs are 0,0,1,0,1.','Only M has output one, and it occurs exactly after the two input pairs 01 ending at positions 3 and 5. Output is determined by state, not directly by the current input.')

T('1-sequential','modulo-counter','Counters','Design and trace a synchronous modulo-six counter',r'''
A counter is a sequential circuit that follows a specified cycle of states on clock edges. A modulo-n counter repeats after n states, so it needs at least ceiling(log₂n) storage bits. That bit count establishes capacity but does not by itself implement the desired sequence. For example, three ordinary binary counter bits naturally count eight states unless logic changes the transition after the sixth state.
In a synchronous design, all flip-flops sample their inputs on the same edge. For D flip-flops, the D inputs represent the desired next-state code. Construct a transition table first, including a rule for unused encodings if recovery is required, and derive each next-state bit from that table. Simultaneous updates mean every new bit is calculated from the same old state, rather than allowing a new lower bit to influence another bit during the edge.
A self-starting counter must recover from every unused state, while a design specified only for a reset start may leave those transitions unconstrained. Ripple counters can have temporary intermediate codes because their clocks cascade; those transient codes differ from the synchronous next-state table. When tracing, count edges carefully: the reset value is the state before the first active edge, not the result of that edge.
''','A synchronous modulo-six counter uses D flip-flops, resets to 000, counts 0 through 5, and sends unused states 110 and 111 to 000. Find its width and state after eight clock edges.',r'Choose storage::b=\lceil\log_2 6\rceil=3::Two bits hold only four states, while three can encode all six required states.~Define wrap and recovery::::The valid sequence is 000→001→010→011→100→101→000. Both unused encodings also transition to 000.~Trace eight edges::8\bmod6=2::Six edges return the reset state; two more edges reach 010.', 'Three flip-flops; after eight edges the state is 010 (decimal 2).','The first six after-edge states are 001,010,011,100,101,000. Edges 7 and 8 then produce 001 and 010, and unused states recover in one edge.')

T('1-representation','fixed-multiply','Arithmetic','Multiply fixed-point values without losing the scale',r'''
Fixed-point arithmetic operates on integer codes whose interpretation includes an implicit power-of-two scale. If a signed code I has f fractional bits, its represented value is I/2ᶠ. Addition of equal-scale operands can directly add codes, subject to overflow, but multiplication changes the scale: codes with f fractional bits each produce a raw product with 2f fractional bits. Keeping the original scale requires dividing the raw integer product by 2ᶠ.
Use a widened signed product before rescaling. Multiplying only at the original storage width can discard high bits before the scale correction, yielding a wrong answer even when the final mathematical value would fit. When rescaling discards nonzero low bits, apply the stated rounding rule; for negative values, truncation toward zero and an arithmetic right shift need not produce the same integer.
After rescaling, check that the final signed code lies within the destination width’s range. Overflow refers to inability to represent the result, while rounding error comes from discarding fractional precision. Verify the calculation by decoding the final code and comparing it to multiplication of the decoded input values, allowing only the rounding error specified by the format.
''','In signed 8-bit fixed point with four fractional bits, multiply values 1.5 and −0.5 using a widened product and store the result at the same scale. No rounding is needed here.',r'Encode operands::I_1=1.5(16)=24,\quad I_2=-0.5(16)=-8::The stored codes represent values divided by 16.~Multiply widened codes::P=24(-8)=-192::The raw product has eight fractional bits, so its value is −192/256.~Restore destination scale::I_{out}=P/16=-12::Dividing by 16 leaves four fractional bits and is exact because P is divisible by 16.~Decode and check::x_{out}=-12/16=-0.75::The signed code −12 fits the eight-bit range, so no overflow occurs.', 'The result is −0.75, stored as code −12 (11110100 in eight-bit two’s complement).','Direct value multiplication gives 1.5×(−0.5)=−0.75; decoding 11110100 gives (244−256)/16=−0.75.')

T('2-alu-control','hardwired-decode','Hardwired control','Derive control signals from encoded controller states',r'''
A hardwired control unit produces datapath control signals using logic gates, a state register and any required instruction decoding. Its state records which part of instruction execution is in progress. The current state and inputs determine the next state, while the control logic asserts signals such as memory read, instruction-register load, ALU operation selection and destination-register write. There is no stored microinstruction word to fetch for each step.
Start from the datapath’s permitted actions and the controller’s state table. Encode each state, build a truth table for each output signal, then simplify the output functions. Several signals can share the same state decode when they are needed together. A minimal Boolean expression still must be checked against every encoded state, including reserved or unused codes under the specified behavior.
State encoding affects the resulting logic: binary encoding saves flip-flops but usually requires more decoding; one-hot encoding uses more state bits and may simplify control equations. Neither encoding alone establishes performance. The key distinction from microprogrammed control is where the sequencing and output decisions are implemented. To verify a hardwired design, substitute each state code into the Boolean outputs and confirm that only the intended datapath actions are enabled.
''','A controller uses q₁q₀=00 for idle, 01 for fetch, 10 for register ADD and 11 for halt. MemRead and IRWrite are asserted only in fetch; ALUAdd and RegWrite only in ADD. Derive the four control equations.',r'Decode fetch::F=\bar q_1q_0::The product is true at state 01 and nowhere else.~Decode execution::E=q_1\bar q_0::The product selects exactly state 10.~Drive controls::MemRead=IRWrite=F,\quad ALUAdd=RegWrite=E::Each paired signal shares the state in which the specified action occurs.~Check inactive states::::At 00 and 11 both F and E are zero, so all four outputs are inactive.', 'MemRead=IRWrite=¬q₁q₀; ALUAdd=RegWrite=q₁¬q₀.','Substitution at 01 enables only fetch controls, at 10 only ADD controls, and at idle/halt none. These are direct combinational state decodes rather than control-store lookups.')

T('2-cache','two-level-hierarchy','Memory hierarchy','Follow conditional accesses through two cache levels',r'''
A memory hierarchy places fast, small storage near the processor and slower, larger storage farther away. A request first checks the nearest cache. A miss sends the request to the next level; only a miss there reaches main memory. Consequently the traffic observed by a lower cache is a subset of the processor’s requests. A local miss rate is conditional on reaching that level, while a global miss rate measures misses as a fraction of all processor requests.
Write down an access tree before computing rates or average time. For two levels, the main-memory request probability is the L1 miss probability multiplied by the L2 local miss probability. If every access pays the L1 lookup time, every L1 miss pays an additional L2 lookup, and every L2 miss pays an additional memory penalty, average time is the sum of each incremental cost weighted by its probability of occurring.
Time conventions matter: an additional penalty excludes earlier work, whereas a complete path time includes it. Mixing those descriptions can count the same lookup twice. Cache capacity and locality affect rates, but the arithmetic must use the rates supplied for the stated workload. Verify a hierarchical calculation using expected request counts over a convenient number of accesses and comparing those counts with the probability model.
''','L1 lookup is 1 ns with miss rate 10%. An L1 miss adds a 4 ns L2 lookup. L2 has a local miss rate 20%; an L2 miss adds 50 ns for memory. For 1000 accesses, find memory requests and AMAT.',r'Trace L1 traffic::N_{L2}=1000(0.10)=100::Only L1 misses reach the second cache.~Trace memory traffic::N_{memory}=100(0.20)=20::The local L2 miss rate applies to its 100 incoming requests, giving a global memory rate 2%.~Weight incremental costs::AMAT=1+0.10(4)+0.10(0.20)(50)=2.4\ ns::Every access pays L1; smaller conditional subsets pay the later costs.', 'Expected memory requests: 20; AMAT: 2.4 ns.','Total expected lookup/penalty time is 1000×1+100×4+20×50=2400 ns; dividing by 1000 yields 2.4 ns.')

T('2-memory-io','io-register-trace','I/O interface','Read status and data through a memory-mapped interface',r'''
An I/O interface provides a defined way for processor instructions to interact with a device. In memory-mapped I/O, some addresses select device registers instead of ordinary RAM. A status register reports conditions such as ready or busy; a data register transfers the payload; a control register may start an operation or select its mode. The instruction still performs a load or store, but the selected device determines what that access means.
Treat device-register semantics as part of the problem specification. Reading a data register can clear a ready flag or consume a queued byte; writing a control register can initiate work. Such accesses may have side effects and cannot generally be reordered or repeated as if they were ordinary memory reads. Polling repeatedly checks status until the specified ready condition holds, then accesses the appropriate data register.
Keep address, register contents and individual status bits separate. A nonzero status value does not necessarily mean the particular ready bit is set, so test the stated mask. Interrupts and DMA change how readiness and transfer work are scheduled, but they still rely on an interface with defined registers or descriptors. Verify a polling trace by explaining which accesses touch status, which consume data, and whether any side effect changes later observations.
''','A memory-mapped device has STATUS at 0x1000 and DATA at 0x1004. STATUS bit 0 indicates ready. Consecutive STATUS reads return 0x04 then 0x05. When ready, DATA returns 0x41 and reading it clears ready. What does the polling routine obtain?',r'Test first status::0x04\mathbin{\&}0x01=0::Another status bit is set, but the ready bit is zero, so the routine keeps polling.~Test second status::0x05\mathbin{\&}0x01=1::The ready bit is now set, permitting a DATA read.~Consume data::::A load from 0x1004 returns byte 0x41 and clears STATUS bit 0 under the stated interface semantics.', 'The routine obtains 0x41 after two status reads and one data read.','0x04 being nonzero is insufficient to signal ready. The data address 0x1004 differs from its returned contents 0x41, and a second read is not guaranteed to return the same byte after ready is cleared.')

T('2-pipeline','forwarding-stages','Forwarding','Trace forwarding and the unavoidable load-use bubble',r'''
Forwarding routes an instruction’s newly produced result directly to a later instruction’s operand input, bypassing register-file writeback. In a five-stage IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB pipeline, an ALU instruction normally produces its result at the end of EX. The next instruction can use that value at EX in the following cycle through an EX/MEM bypass. This removes a read-after-write delay only when the required value exists before the consumer uses it.
A load produces its data at the end of MEM. An immediately following instruction would reach EX in that same cycle, too early to use a value that arrives only at its end. Under this conventional timing model, one stall holds the consumer in ID and inserts an EX bubble. The consumer then uses the loaded data in EX during the next cycle via forwarding. The pipeline’s exact bypass paths and timing assumptions must be specified before counting stalls.
Draw a stage trace, mark when each producer generates its result and when each consumer needs it, then delay only dependencies that lack a timely path. Later ALU-to-ALU dependencies may still need no extra bubble after a load-use stall. Total cycles equal ideal fill-and-drain cycles plus actual inserted stall cycles; a forwarding benefit should never be assumed to create a value earlier than its producing stage.
''','Use a five-stage, single-issue in-order pipeline with full EX-input forwarding. ALU results are ready at end of EX, loads at end of MEM; data cannot arrive and be consumed in the same cycle. Trace LW r1,0(r0); ADD r2,r1,r3; SUB r4,r2,r5 from an empty pipeline.',r'Trace the load::::LW uses IF1, ID2, EX3, MEM4 and WB5. Its r1 value becomes available only at the end of cycle 4.~Insert load-use stall::::ADD has IF2 and ID3, stays in ID during cycle 4, then uses EX5, MEM6 and WB7. The bubble prevents its planned EX4 from reading r1 before it exists.~Forward the ALU result::::SUB has IF3, is held during cycle 4, then ID5, EX6, MEM7 and WB8. ADD produces r2 at the end of EX5, so EX/MEM forwarding supplies SUB in EX6 without another stall.~Count completion::C=5+3-1+1=8::Three instructions need seven ideal cycles plus the single load-use bubble.', 'One stall; final completion is cycle 8. LW→ADD uses load-data forwarding after the bubble, and ADD→SUB uses ALU-result forwarding without a second bubble.','Without the bubble, ADD would require r1 during EX4 while LW only produces it at the end of MEM4. With the bubble, ADD’s EX5 and SUB’s EX6 each have their input value available beforehand.')
